WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.380
So our next segment will
contain two speakers

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:20.350
speaking about data in
astrophysics as well

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.950
as data in genomics.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:26.020
The first speaker
will be speaking

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:30.730
about the Open Data Challenge
in Computational Astrophysics.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:33.040
Dr. Daisuke Nagai is
an associate professor

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.336
of physics and astronomy
at Yale University

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:37.960
and the faculty
co-director of the Yale

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:41.060
Center for Research Computing.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.150
His current research focuses on
theoretical and computational

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.010
modeling of the
structure formation

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:49.596
of the universe and its
application to cosmology.

00:00:49.596 --> 00:00:55.090
He obtained his PhD from the
University of Chicago in 2005.

00:00:55.090 --> 00:00:59.740
It was the Sherman Fairchild
Postdoctoral Scholar

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.960
at the California
Institute of Technology

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.560
before he joined the
Yale faculty in 2008.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:06.360
Welcome, Dr. Nagai.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:08.848
Thank you very much.

00:01:08.848 --> 00:01:12.320
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:24.211 --> 00:01:24.710
Great.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:26.584
Well, thank you very
much for the invitation.

00:01:26.584 --> 00:01:29.480
It's a pleasure to actually talk
about some of the perspectives

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.670
that we're actually learning
from the computational

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.040
astrophysics area.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.450
So I would like to start.

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:39.170
I think this is a really
exciting time in science

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:42.650
where we might be actually going
through some sort of paradigm

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:44.720
shift in science.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.510
If you look back in the
history, science has been--

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:49.880
over a long time, the
thousands of years,

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:52.730
it's mostly the
empirical studies

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:56.180
of describing natural
phenomena by collecting data.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:59.240
There was a paradigm shift
several hundred years ago

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.820
when the theoretical
branches arose

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:05.270
where Isaac Newton
actually started

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.760
to use mathematical formulations
to actually describe

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:10.940
laws of nature and tried
in the hope of describing

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:14.690
the empirical data that the
astronomers were collecting.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:17.340
And that opened up
that theoretical branch

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.550
several hundred years
ago and since then, we've

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:23.630
seen the emergence of this
field of physics, which

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:28.670
has identified several
laws of physics

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:31.010
from electromagnetism
to gravitational physics

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:33.380
to quantum theory,
which has been

00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:36.700
teaching us a lot about the
physical laws of nature.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.140
But in the last few
decades, we have actually

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.940
seen another emergence
of a new technique.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.540
This is the
computational branch.

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.680
And then today, due to the
emergence of computation,

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.250
that's actually starting to
produce a large amount of data

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:54.920
that this is maybe
a new paradigm

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:58.410
shift that maybe the data is
actually telling us something.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.370
And this new shift
actually requires new ways

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.270
of thinking to do science.

00:03:05.270 --> 00:03:08.300
The computation permeates
throughout the society,

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.810
starting all the way from the
smallest scales of on particle

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:14.510
physics all the way
up to the structure

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:15.600
formation of the universe.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.690
And everything
happens in between,

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:20.330
from molecular dynamics
and applications

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:24.890
to drug discovery to
looking for fusion research

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:27.620
in the hopes of actually
finding clean energy--

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:28.280
car safety.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:33.980
These days, in order to study
the safety of an automobile,

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.110
we no longer actually
crash the car

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:37.880
but we do computational
simulations

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:42.180
to get insights into how to
actually build a safer car.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.360
If you want to
actually train a pilot,

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.682
we would actually use
the flight simulator

00:03:46.682 --> 00:03:48.140
to actually train
the pilot instead

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:52.490
of having them actually fly
on the actual physical plane

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:53.690
and so forth.

00:03:53.690 --> 00:03:57.050
And on the much larger
scale, the weather forecast,

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:59.030
planet formation,
galaxy formation,

00:03:59.030 --> 00:04:01.070
and large-scale
structure formation,

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.710
these are the phenomena that
are very difficult to actually

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:06.620
study and the
computation approach

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:10.610
has been really revolutionizing
the way we actually

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:12.320
understand those systems.

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.250
So using computation,
what we're actually doing

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.017
is actually trying to
give a complete evolution

00:04:19.017 --> 00:04:20.600
of the physical
system, where you have

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:21.950
a parameter that you can vary.

00:04:21.950 --> 00:04:25.820
And we are starting to do the
experiment using computers.

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:29.690
And this is sort of the new
way of actually doing science

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:32.660
and as the computer
gets faster and faster,

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:35.870
the data is growing with
an exponential rate.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:38.270
And with that larger
data, it's even

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.300
starting to change
how we actually

00:04:41.300 --> 00:04:42.350
think about the science.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:43.766
Typically, we
develop a hypothesis

00:04:43.766 --> 00:04:47.195
and go out and collect the
data to test the hypothesis.

00:04:47.195 --> 00:04:49.684
But however, when you
have so much data,

00:04:49.684 --> 00:04:52.100
you can start to think about
whether the data can actually

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:55.100
teach us something
that we don't know.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.320
Could the data teach
us more about the ideas

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:58.780
that we could generate?

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.430
Those are possibilities
that were potential

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.400
that is actually making us
making this field particularly

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:05.390
exciting.

00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:09.430
Data is becoming
increasingly open and public

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:12.680
and the fact that the
data is everywhere

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:15.800
is actually bringing people
from physical sciences

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.720
to life sciences
to medical sciences

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:21.930
together, connected through
statistics and computing.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:27.170
So we might actually be
in this rare opportunity

00:05:27.170 --> 00:05:30.410
where we can actually
exploit this paradigm shift

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:33.200
and what comes out
is I think what

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.330
we're here to actually discuss.

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:39.620
How can we actually make
this feel the most fruitful

00:05:39.620 --> 00:05:40.760
as possible?

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:44.000
And my area of astrophysics
has been inherently

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.820
and traditionally data-driven
because we cannot do

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:51.560
experiments on the universe that
we are interested in knowing.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:54.650
So what's on the left-hand side
is the Planck Satellite mission

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:58.430
that's in space observing
the universe in microwaves.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:01.640
What's on the right-hand side
is the Sloan Digital Sky Survey

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.270
studying the galaxies'
distributions in optical.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:08.750
And I sort of think of this
as a cosmic genome project,

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:11.690
where we are actually mapping
out the distribution of mass

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.550
in the entire universe.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:16.194
In the microwave, you can study
about the distribution of mass

00:06:16.194 --> 00:06:18.110
in the very early phase
of the universe, where

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:20.044
the universe was very smooth.

00:06:20.044 --> 00:06:21.710
And what's shown on
the right-hand side,

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:27.470
the pictures of distributions
of galaxies in the entire sky,

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:29.870
is actually showing the
universe is very clumpy--

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:30.581
very homogeneous.

00:06:30.581 --> 00:06:32.330
There are regions with
a very high density

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:33.788
concentration of
galaxies and there

00:06:33.788 --> 00:06:37.680
are regions with empty voids.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.240
And so we have a snapshot
of how the universe evolved

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:45.140
from the very smooth state
to a very clumpy state.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:48.060
But in this field, we
also know the physical law

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:51.990
that's governing the devolutions
of this physical system.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.990
On the largest possible scale,
the gravity is a dominant force

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.870
and we have a working model
of the gravitational laws

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:00.024
developed by Einstein.

00:07:00.024 --> 00:07:01.440
So here, what we're
doing is we're

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:05.280
taking the map that we
see in the early universe

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:06.780
from the cosmic
microwave background

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:08.370
as an initial condition.

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:10.960
You can then set that
initial condition

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:13.350
and solve Einstein's
equation forward in time

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.270
and you should be
able to reproduce

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:17.250
the distribution of
a clumpy universe

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:20.520
in the present-day universe.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.980
So this is what has been
done and it actually

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:27.300
generates the universe that
consists of the gravitationally

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.330
bound objects like galaxies
and clusters of galaxies.

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:32.730
And also, there are
empty regions like voids,

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:35.340
as we see in the data.

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:37.620
So what we're really
doing, modern astrophysics

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:41.310
is really the nexus of the
big data and supercomputing.

00:07:41.310 --> 00:07:44.700
On one side, we have a
telescope as an instrument which

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:47.490
is taking data,
taking a snapshot

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:49.950
at any given point in space.

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:52.380
But however, on
the left-hand side,

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:55.160
we have the
high-performance computer.

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:57.100
It also acts as an instrument.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.310
In fact, we do most of
the experiment where

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:05.190
we vary the [? knobs ?] in using
this high-performance computer.

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:07.620
Both of these instruments
generate a huge amount

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:11.130
of data, which has to be
analyzed through the data

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:12.390
analysis pipeline.

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:14.460
You use statistics
to actually compute

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:16.470
the relevant
statistical quantity.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.870
You glue those
information together

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:21.480
through the statistical
framework of Markov chain Monte

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.180
Carlo and then try
to infer things

00:08:24.180 --> 00:08:27.060
like what the universe
actually consists of.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:29.790
Today, we believe the
universe that best

00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:37.650
fits the observation actually
consists of the 4% of stuff

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:40.980
that we knew about.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:43.380
These are matters,
protons, and electrons.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:46.560
And it turns out that
this 96% of the stuff that

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.930
fills the universe
is made out of things

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:50.820
that we didn't know about.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:54.330
This field of studying the
universe in this kind of way

00:08:54.330 --> 00:08:59.340
has really taught us that
all the physics that we

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:05.970
knew until the last century only
made up 4%, really opening up

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:07.950
a new avenue for
discovering what's

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.450
the nature of dark
matter, what's

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:12.210
the nature of dark energy,
what is the universe made of,

00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:14.780
and where we are going.

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:16.790
Now, to do this, on
the left-hand side,

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:18.980
on the computational
modeling, there

00:09:18.980 --> 00:09:22.370
are different types of code
available to actually model

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:25.070
the physical evolutions
of the universe.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:28.940
And here, I'm showing the
six different types of code.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:32.480
All of these are solving
the same set of equations

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:34.850
by using different techniques.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:38.120
Some are representing
the distribution of mass

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:38.930
as a particle.

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:42.560
Some represent the distribution
of mass as a grid and cells

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:47.010
and you solve the same set of
equations in different ways.

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:54.650
And so in order to actually
test how the code is actually

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:57.980
working, you try to use
the different types of code

00:09:57.980 --> 00:09:59.510
and solve the same problem.

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.480
This is a simple problem where
you have a gas blob moving

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:06.180
in a tenuous medium and you
use the five different codes

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:09.440
to actually solve the
exact same problem.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:11.810
And the interesting thing--

00:10:11.810 --> 00:10:15.200
well, the problem
here is that depending

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:18.290
on what kind of code or
algorithm that you use,

00:10:18.290 --> 00:10:21.200
the evolution of
this physical system

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:23.610
produces different results.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:26.780
So this is sort of the
reproducibility crisis

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:28.610
that we are facing
in astrophysics,

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:30.500
that the computational
results do

00:10:30.500 --> 00:10:33.710
depend on code and algorithm.

00:10:33.710 --> 00:10:37.390
So for any given algorithm, you
can reproduce the same results.

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:39.470
But although you're
solving the same problem,

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:41.810
if you're solving them
using a different technique,

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:44.690
it could result in
different results.

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:47.360
Here is a slightly more
complicated situation

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:50.390
where we're using the 12
different types of code.

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:54.560
So for computational
astrophysics, what's important

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:57.679
is to do this thing called
"a code comparison project."

00:10:57.679 --> 00:10:59.720
You need to develop
different types of approaches

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:01.700
for solving the same
problem and starting

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:03.327
with the exact same
initial condition

00:11:03.327 --> 00:11:05.660
and trying to solve a slightly
more complicated problem,

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:08.210
in this case, the formation
of galaxy clusters,

00:11:08.210 --> 00:11:11.830
the most massive
objects in the universe.

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:14.280
So here's an
attempt back in 1999

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:18.202
where the 12 simulators
got together and decided

00:11:18.202 --> 00:11:20.160
to simulate the same
thing, since they were not

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.170
agreeing among
themselves on the results

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:25.770
that they were getting
on the same problem.

00:11:25.770 --> 00:11:30.480
And you can see the
distributions of gas

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.960
in these massive
galaxy clusters and you

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:37.620
can see more or less that
they actually form something.

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:39.750
They are a spherical blob
but there are clearly

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:41.580
some differences, too.

00:11:41.580 --> 00:11:43.440
What's more notorious
here is here,

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:47.580
I'm plotting these distributions
of gas as a function of radius

00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:52.530
and the results actually
agree fairly well

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:54.046
at the very large radii.

00:11:54.046 --> 00:11:55.420
But there are
notable differences

00:11:55.420 --> 00:11:58.420
in the inner region,
depending, again, on the code.

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:01.230
Whether you use grid-based
or particle-based code,

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:03.910
you get systematically
different results.

00:12:03.910 --> 00:12:05.760
So this kind of
comparison can actually

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:10.960
teach us about the pros and
cons of different techniques.

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:13.740
So the scientific results,
especially computational

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:16.560
results, must be not
only reproducible

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.270
but it would also
have to be accurate.

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:22.020
This also raises the
question as to how

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:24.120
do we actually know
that we are converging

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:31.380
on the right answer, which
is also important, as well.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:36.170
Now, the reproduces challenges--

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:38.590
so so far, I talked about
how to run a simulation.

00:12:38.590 --> 00:12:41.960
Using different types of code,
you get different results.

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:43.920
The computational
astrophysics involve

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:47.270
fairly complex workflow, where
you have to go through and take

00:12:47.270 --> 00:12:49.030
the simulation out,
put the visualized in,

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:52.611
then analyze through many,
many layers of steps.

00:12:52.611 --> 00:12:54.860
First, identify the object
that you are interested in.

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:56.499
How you define the object--

00:12:56.499 --> 00:12:58.040
how do you actually
analyze the data?

00:12:58.040 --> 00:13:01.280
There are fairly
complicated algorithms

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:03.980
that go through the
process those outputs

00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:07.790
of that simulation and then go
through the analysis pipeline.

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:11.150
Finally, plot the results
of the interested quantity.

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:13.550
So the complex workflow--

00:13:13.550 --> 00:13:17.540
we also need to identify the
different types and levels

00:13:17.540 --> 00:13:22.700
of reproducibility and accuracy
in this research process

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:24.691
in order to get
the robust results.

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:31.360
So in this process, most of
the intellectual contributions

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:34.510
and assets actually are
encoded in that code, which

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:37.330
consists of two
things, an algorithm

00:13:37.330 --> 00:13:40.990
that we use to actually
develop our software

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:42.930
and the software itself.

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:46.360
So the code is where most of
the intellectual contributions

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:49.450
are and then the
additional contribution

00:13:49.450 --> 00:13:53.740
lies in the workflow of
the code that interconnects

00:13:53.740 --> 00:13:56.080
between different types of
code at different stages

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:57.920
of the research.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:13:59.920
Some of the solutions are
specific to a research

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:03.560
community, such as
algorithms and software.

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:06.370
However, there are
also common solutions.

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:09.730
Those include the software
sharing and preservation,

00:14:09.730 --> 00:14:14.110
which we use GitHub and
Bitbucket to actually do

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:16.450
that software
development as a team--

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:19.990
also with the scientists
around the world.

00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:24.340
And in terms of data sharing,
we use Globus Online extensively

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:26.650
to disseminate the
outputs of the simulations

00:14:26.650 --> 00:14:29.640
and share them around with
scientists around the world.

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:31.630
The data science
policy is another area

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:34.540
where there are
some commonalities

00:14:34.540 --> 00:14:38.110
among the researchers
who do research

00:14:38.110 --> 00:14:39.850
on different disciplines.

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:43.000
Those include the e-journals,
funding agencies--

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:45.799
infrastructure are some of
the things that we share.

00:14:45.799 --> 00:14:47.590
Even though you're
trying to actually solve

00:14:47.590 --> 00:14:50.290
a different problem, there are
things that we can actually

00:14:50.290 --> 00:14:51.920
coordinate.

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:53.800
So the Yale Center for
Research and Computing

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:56.290
can actually address some
of these issues that are

00:14:56.290 --> 00:15:02.590
common among the researchers.

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:04.480
And those include the
cyber infrastructure,

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:08.320
such as HPC and storage,
software, education,

00:15:08.320 --> 00:15:11.770
and training and grant
support and community

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:15.580
outreach, including day of data
and organizing and bringing

00:15:15.580 --> 00:15:18.550
researchers together from
the different disciplines

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:22.330
to actually talk about the
data or things that are common.

00:15:22.330 --> 00:15:24.400
In this way, we can
improve the efficiency

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:26.980
and actually exchange the
different techniques that

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:28.880
are used and different ideas.

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:30.880
And I think this
is the way forward

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:38.890
in terms of making the
transformative impact

00:15:38.890 --> 00:15:40.630
in different fields.

00:15:40.630 --> 00:15:42.880
So let me just
conclude by saying

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:47.140
that both reproducibility
and accuracy are

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:48.985
important for credibility
of any science.

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:55.210
Open data and open
code are certainly

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:56.520
the important solution.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:15:58.450
Or in our computational
astrophysics,

00:15:58.450 --> 00:16:01.510
we are also learning that
the algorithm and software

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:04.540
development is an
equally important aspect

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:07.750
of doing science in this area.

00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:10.390
And we can use
combinations of those

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:13.090
as a basis for
data-driven science

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:14.650
hopefully in the future.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:18.100
Some of the solutions are
specific to the research

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:22.330
community or even sometimes to
the group, code development,

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:24.250
process, and workflow.

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:26.560
In this area, the
discipline-specific data

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:29.370
scientists can actually
play a very important role

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:35.410
in developing and in optimizing
some of these software

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:37.850
development and also the
efficiency of the process

00:16:37.850 --> 00:16:39.730
and the workflow.

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:41.230
Some of the other
solutions that are

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:44.370
more common among the
different research communities

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:48.520
include HPC infrastructure,
software, and data and code

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:49.540
sharing.

00:16:49.540 --> 00:16:51.910
And those are the
things that the Yale

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:55.210
Center for Research and
Computing will be able to help.

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:56.890
However, there is
some missing gap

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:59.480
that I notice as
we actually develop

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:02.110
trying to figure out
what kind of research

00:17:02.110 --> 00:17:04.690
should be done within our own
group-- what kind of things

00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:06.579
should we actually
coordinate and do it

00:17:06.579 --> 00:17:08.391
as a part of the community.

00:17:08.391 --> 00:17:09.849
Another important
thing that we are

00:17:09.849 --> 00:17:12.220
starting to realize
in our research

00:17:12.220 --> 00:17:14.770
is the algorithm development.

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:17.980
As you develop a code,
as I highlighted,

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:20.680
the computational
results could actually

00:17:20.680 --> 00:17:23.740
depend on not just what
problem you're solving

00:17:23.740 --> 00:17:26.410
but how you're
solving that problem.

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:30.790
And we sometimes do not know.

00:17:30.790 --> 00:17:32.841
Basically, the problem
that we are facing

00:17:32.841 --> 00:17:34.840
is that you're solving
the same set of equations

00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:38.860
and it's well-tested by
solving the problem that we

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:40.030
know the answers to.

00:17:40.030 --> 00:17:42.340
But we're taking that
code and extrapolating it

00:17:42.340 --> 00:17:46.390
to the region where we don't
know how well the code actually

00:17:46.390 --> 00:17:47.350
performs.

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:49.840
Different types of algorithms
have pros and cons so

00:17:49.840 --> 00:17:55.030
when you actually extrapolate
the code into the regime

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:56.980
where we haven't tested--

00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:58.480
but this is the
whole point of doing

00:17:58.480 --> 00:18:01.930
the computational modeling is
that that opens up solutions

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:05.470
to some of the problems that
have not been solved before.

00:18:05.470 --> 00:18:09.994
But it's actually difficult
to convince ourselves,

00:18:09.994 --> 00:18:11.410
how do we know
that we're actually

00:18:11.410 --> 00:18:14.500
converging to the true answer?

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:16.860
Given the code, you can
reproduce the results.

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:18.970
But if you use different
code, you actually

00:18:18.970 --> 00:18:20.630
find two different results.

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:22.555
In the case of the
galaxy cluster where

00:18:22.555 --> 00:18:28.210
we see differences in the
grid-based and particle-based

00:18:28.210 --> 00:18:33.610
code, there is a long debate
about which one is actually

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:34.660
the right solution.

00:18:34.660 --> 00:18:36.780
We just get the two
different solutions

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:40.480
and we don't know which one
is the correct solution.

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:44.950
The problem is very serious
because the comparison--

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:46.930
I show two comparisons.

00:18:46.930 --> 00:18:48.850
Those are separated by 15 years.

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:51.130
We're still debating
about the same problem

00:18:51.130 --> 00:18:54.340
after 15 years of research.

00:18:54.340 --> 00:18:57.400
And this has to do with
the-- and the only way

00:18:57.400 --> 00:18:59.929
we can move forward is the
independent development

00:18:59.929 --> 00:19:01.720
of different types of
code, different types

00:19:01.720 --> 00:19:03.970
of algorithms, and
then the community

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:08.890
has to convince themselves a
better understanding of pros

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:11.590
and cons, the characterization
of the code algorithm,

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:16.720
is actually the way forward in
actually getting a true answer

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:18.262
from computational astrophysics.

00:19:18.262 --> 00:19:19.720
And this is important
because we're

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:23.260
using computational
models to infer

00:19:23.260 --> 00:19:26.360
some important results,
such as the composition

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:27.880
and origins of the universe.

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:30.790
And if we're using the wrong
models to actually interpret

00:19:30.790 --> 00:19:33.070
the data where we cannot
do the experiment,

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:37.390
we will be inferring
the wrong results.

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:39.460
So this is one area that
I think we would need--

00:19:39.460 --> 00:19:46.030
to actually make a real
impact, we need not just the--

00:19:46.030 --> 00:19:51.190
it's important to actually make
the data and code available.

00:19:51.190 --> 00:19:56.170
But it's also important to
think about these issues

00:19:56.170 --> 00:19:59.890
of the accuracy and how we
actually convince ourselves

00:19:59.890 --> 00:20:02.930
that results we're getting
are the right answer.

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:05.430
So let me stop here and
thank you very much.

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:09.350
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:22.980
Thank you, Dr. Nagai.

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:26.520
We will have Q&amp;A for both
