Yes.

00:21:40.166 --> 00:21:42.158
Maybe [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:42.158 --> 00:21:44.150
Yeah.

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:45.146
All right.

00:21:45.146 --> 00:21:47.138
Thank you.

00:21:47.138 --> 00:21:50.126
OK.

00:21:50.126 --> 00:21:51.620
OK.

00:21:51.620 --> 00:21:53.114
Thanks.

00:21:53.114 --> 00:21:56.102
OK.

00:21:56.102 --> 00:21:57.594
Yeah.

00:21:57.594 --> 00:21:58.094
Right.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:03.660
Hi, everyone.

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:05.680
It's a great
pleasure to be here.

00:22:05.680 --> 00:22:08.860
I want to thank the organizers
for giving me this opportunity.

00:22:08.860 --> 00:22:10.830
So I'm going to switch
gears a little bit

00:22:10.830 --> 00:22:16.770
and I'm going to talk about
computational privacy.

00:22:16.770 --> 00:22:18.300
It may get a little
bit technical

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:24.930
but I'll try to get through
it as easy as possible.

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:27.570
So yeah, the title
is "Quantification

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:31.230
of Sensitive Information
Leakage in Phenotype Data."

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:37.450
So let me try to give you
guys a brief context here.

00:22:37.450 --> 00:22:41.110
So privacy is obviously
very important.

00:22:41.110 --> 00:22:45.460
And as we do more open science
and we share more data,

00:22:45.460 --> 00:22:47.800
one problem that
is going to come up

00:22:47.800 --> 00:22:50.740
is a lot of different
data sets that

00:22:50.740 --> 00:22:53.350
contain sensitive information
or personal information,

00:22:53.350 --> 00:22:55.570
that they can be
linked together.

00:22:55.570 --> 00:22:58.840
And if you think about
an adversarial person,

00:22:58.840 --> 00:23:01.420
he may go and fetch
those data sets

00:23:01.420 --> 00:23:06.160
and well, he can invade privacy.

00:23:06.160 --> 00:23:08.880
So I guess one thing that
we will need to think about

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:11.440
in the next several
years is going

00:23:11.440 --> 00:23:14.140
to be how we can
quantify the risks

00:23:14.140 --> 00:23:18.190
and what's the
sense of information

00:23:18.190 --> 00:23:20.920
leakage in these datasets.

00:23:20.920 --> 00:23:22.550
It's not a solved
problem at all.

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:25.120
We need to think about
it really carefully.

00:23:25.120 --> 00:23:29.890
And actually, NIH wants to
push data sharing a lot more

00:23:29.890 --> 00:23:32.550
in the next several years.

00:23:32.550 --> 00:23:35.050
And actually, yesterday, I don't
know if you guys follow it.

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:41.210
There was this 21st Century
Cures passed through the House

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:43.690
and actually, that
gives NIH the incentives

00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:48.370
to actually make researchers put
their data in the central data

00:23:48.370 --> 00:23:53.740
commons that NIH is
actually planning to produce

00:23:53.740 --> 00:23:55.250
in a couple of years.

00:23:55.250 --> 00:23:57.610
So these are going to be
real challenges that we

00:23:57.610 --> 00:23:59.350
will face in a couple of years.

00:23:59.350 --> 00:24:02.860
And I would want to
start with this example

00:24:02.860 --> 00:24:04.040
that we like to present.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:06.220
It has nothing to
do with genomics.

00:24:06.220 --> 00:24:08.530
So there was this
Netflix price challenge

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:10.227
that this Netflix company--

00:24:10.227 --> 00:24:11.560
I'm sure you guys know about it.

00:24:11.560 --> 00:24:17.260
It's the movie rental
company where these guys,

00:24:17.260 --> 00:24:20.590
they wanted to improve their
movie recommendation systems.

00:24:20.590 --> 00:24:25.630
And what they did was from the
huge database of movie ratings,

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:27.670
they extracted this
training dataset

00:24:27.670 --> 00:24:30.880
which consisted of these users.

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:32.070
Well, these are real people.

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:34.900
Well, their names are
obviously de-anonymized.

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:38.650
And there were these movies
and what this guy rated this,

00:24:38.650 --> 00:24:42.010
when did this guy rate this
movie, and this movie's rating.

00:24:42.010 --> 00:24:43.370
So higher is better.

00:24:43.370 --> 00:24:45.280
Lower is worse.

00:24:45.280 --> 00:24:48.580
And the idea was the researchers
would take this training

00:24:48.580 --> 00:24:51.560
data set, build machine
learning models,

00:24:51.560 --> 00:24:55.240
and they would then
test these models

00:24:55.240 --> 00:25:01.540
on another testing data set
where the rating is taken out

00:25:01.540 --> 00:25:05.170
so that Netflix can go
and cross-validate and see

00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:06.100
who wins.

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:09.730
And the prize of this
challenge was $1 million

00:25:09.730 --> 00:25:11.590
so it was a pretty
serious thing.

00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:14.140
And it went on for
three years, I think.

00:25:14.140 --> 00:25:17.330
And let me talk about
this dataset a little bit.

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:21.040
So there are 100 million
ratings for 400,000 users.

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:24.670
On average, each user rated
200 movies and each movie

00:25:24.670 --> 00:25:28.360
was rated by 5,000 individuals.

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:30.970
And it turns out this
was not a very good idea

00:25:30.970 --> 00:25:35.480
because these researchers,
Narayanan and Shmatikov,

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:38.170
they actually showed that
you can take this Netflix

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:40.870
dataset which was anonymized
and you can actually

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:45.460
go to another completely
different movie dataset, IMDB,

00:25:45.460 --> 00:25:47.140
and you can actually link these.

00:25:47.140 --> 00:25:50.080
And using pretty
straightforward techniques--

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:53.890
I won't go into the
details of those--

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:57.160
you can actually
identify who a lot

00:25:57.160 --> 00:26:01.060
of the people in Netflix's
dataset, their identities,

00:26:01.060 --> 00:26:02.770
taken from the IMDB.

00:26:02.770 --> 00:26:06.760
So that was a big problem and
actually, Netflix got sued

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:10.600
and they had to pay a
pretty big price for this.

00:26:10.600 --> 00:26:15.040
And well, there are four
basic things to note here.

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:17.950
So the reason why this
attack happened firstly was

00:26:17.950 --> 00:26:19.770
these are very big datasets.

00:26:19.770 --> 00:26:22.750
So if you think
about it, if somebody

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:25.300
has an account on Netflix,
there is a very good likelihood

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:28.810
that he or she is going to
have an account on IMDB.

00:26:28.810 --> 00:26:32.339
And a lot of the users,
they rate a movie.

00:26:32.339 --> 00:26:33.630
For example, you watch a movie.

00:26:33.630 --> 00:26:35.296
Then you would wait
around the same time

00:26:35.296 --> 00:26:36.970
and you would give
similar ratings

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:39.715
and these researchers actually
statistically showed that.

00:26:39.715 --> 00:26:41.825
Well, actually, they also
did the actual attack

00:26:41.825 --> 00:26:43.450
but they showed that
statistically, you

00:26:43.450 --> 00:26:48.430
can link any very big dataset
with a lot of features

00:26:48.430 --> 00:26:49.070
to each other.

00:26:49.070 --> 00:26:51.910
So it wasn't a very
good idea, after all.

00:26:51.910 --> 00:26:55.360
Going into this
phenotypic problem,

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:56.980
phenotype-genotype
linking problem

00:26:56.980 --> 00:26:59.500
that we have, the way we
are thinking about this

00:26:59.500 --> 00:27:01.920
is we have a lot of
phenotype datasets.

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:05.440
These can be your
electronic health records

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:08.770
or it can be a lot of
molecular phenotypes, like gene

00:27:08.770 --> 00:27:12.690
expression levels, metabolimics,
proteomics, and any "omics"

00:27:12.690 --> 00:27:15.100
that you can think about.

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:16.870
And there are these
genotype datasets

00:27:16.870 --> 00:27:19.960
which you might have
gotten in 23andMe

00:27:19.960 --> 00:27:22.510
or some ancestry website
or your hospital.

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:25.840
These are going to get very,
very relevant to our health

00:27:25.840 --> 00:27:26.740
system.

00:27:26.740 --> 00:27:30.700
And what we are
thinking is going

00:27:30.700 --> 00:27:32.860
to be very relevant to
what an adversary does

00:27:32.860 --> 00:27:36.640
is linking these datasets
because recently, I

00:27:36.640 --> 00:27:39.940
think 23andMe, they announced
that they genotyped their 1

00:27:39.940 --> 00:27:40.990
millionth customer.

00:27:40.990 --> 00:27:43.174
So they have millions of
people in their database

00:27:43.174 --> 00:27:44.590
and there's a very
high likelihood

00:27:44.590 --> 00:27:47.830
that the guy who
gets their genotypes

00:27:47.830 --> 00:27:50.500
measured in 23andMe, they're
going to go and do it

00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:54.910
in ancestry.com, too,
because a lot of people

00:27:54.910 --> 00:27:56.290
like doing this stuff.

00:27:56.290 --> 00:27:59.930
But the 23andMe and ancestry.com
don't know about each other.

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:03.920
So if a hacker goes and
hacks these two datasets,

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:07.210
there is a very good likelihood
that he can go and link them

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:11.530
with another data set that
he gets from a hospital

00:28:11.530 --> 00:28:14.232
or somewhere.

00:28:14.232 --> 00:28:15.690
So in our case,
what's happening is

00:28:15.690 --> 00:28:17.590
we have the phenotype
dataset, the genotype

00:28:17.590 --> 00:28:21.240
dataset-- these are
independently served.

00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:23.820
So the genotype there,
we're assuming it's hacked.

00:28:23.820 --> 00:28:26.010
And if a phenotype
dataset is anonymized,

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:29.890
we have these just IDs and
very sensitive information--

00:28:29.890 --> 00:28:31.719
for example, HIV status--

00:28:31.719 --> 00:28:33.260
and a lot of phenotypic
measurements.

00:28:33.260 --> 00:28:35.857
So these phenotyping
measurements are some numbers.

00:28:35.857 --> 00:28:37.190
It can be your gene expressions.

00:28:37.190 --> 00:28:38.106
It can be your height.

00:28:38.106 --> 00:28:40.320
It can be your
blood glucose level

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:42.170
or some other measurement.

00:28:42.170 --> 00:28:44.250
And these are
variants so "0, 1, 2"

00:28:44.250 --> 00:28:50.160
means you are a reference allele
or heterozygous or homozygous

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:50.830
alternate.

00:28:50.830 --> 00:28:51.954
So don't worry about those.

00:28:51.954 --> 00:28:55.970
But these are basically three
valued levels of your genotypes

00:28:55.970 --> 00:28:58.290
at certain positions
in your genome.

00:28:58.290 --> 00:29:02.686
And there are these
public datasets called

00:29:02.686 --> 00:29:04.560
"phenotype-genotype
correlation" they assess.

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:06.960
So what's given
here is a phenotype

00:29:06.960 --> 00:29:08.130
is correlated to a variant.

00:29:08.130 --> 00:29:11.040
So for example, the position
in your genome changes

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:12.610
and your eye color changes.

00:29:12.610 --> 00:29:14.655
So if I look at your eye color--

00:29:14.655 --> 00:29:15.750
this is green--

00:29:15.750 --> 00:29:18.810
I have a very good idea of what
your variant in that position

00:29:18.810 --> 00:29:20.370
is going to be.

00:29:20.370 --> 00:29:22.950
And then what we are
saying is you can actually

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:27.060
do a genotype prediction using
these correlation datasets

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:29.490
because I can go from
this phenotype measurement

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:30.960
to this variant.

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:34.880
But this step is
stochastic so there's

00:29:34.880 --> 00:29:37.310
going to be some errors
that you're making

00:29:37.310 --> 00:29:38.860
when you're getting this--

00:29:38.860 --> 00:29:41.160
I don't know if you guys
can see the laser pointer--

00:29:41.160 --> 00:29:42.767
the predicted variant genotype.

00:29:42.767 --> 00:29:44.600
So there's going to be
a lot of errors here.

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:48.430
But the errors actually don't--

00:29:48.430 --> 00:29:50.180
the attacker doesn't
care about the errors

00:29:50.180 --> 00:29:51.410
that he makes in the genotypes.

00:29:51.410 --> 00:29:53.330
He's trying to link this
dataset to this one.

00:29:53.330 --> 00:29:56.780
So then what happens is he
does genotype comparison

00:29:56.780 --> 00:30:01.850
between these two datasets
and matches the phenotype IDs.

00:30:01.850 --> 00:30:05.270
Our genotype IDs are actually
the identifying information

00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:10.650
and he gets, referring to
genotype ID, the HIV status.

00:30:10.650 --> 00:30:13.850
So at the end of the day,
he linked this genotype ID

00:30:13.850 --> 00:30:18.520
to this HIV status and that's
where the damage is done.

00:30:18.520 --> 00:30:20.820
So I will focus on these.

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:22.320
So what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:28.360
to do an example of how easily
this attack can be done.

00:30:28.360 --> 00:30:32.280
And I'm going to concentrate on
these things called "expression

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:34.170
quantitative trait loci."

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:37.020
That's a long way of saying the
genotype affects our expression

00:30:37.020 --> 00:30:38.530
level.

00:30:38.530 --> 00:30:44.046
So here again, on the x-axis,
we have the genotype is 0, 1--

00:30:44.046 --> 00:30:45.280
2.

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:48.870
As this value increases or
as you go from the reference

00:30:48.870 --> 00:30:51.610
allele to the alternate allele,
your expression level changes.

00:30:51.610 --> 00:30:54.210
So you can think about
this like where you have

00:30:54.210 --> 00:30:56.940
aa, ac, and cc values here.

00:30:56.940 --> 00:31:00.510
So as you change from one
allele to the other one,

00:31:00.510 --> 00:31:02.730
your gene expression
level increases

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:07.156
and a variant changes the gene
expression level of a gene.

00:31:07.156 --> 00:31:10.810
And so what we want to
do is we want to quantify

00:31:10.810 --> 00:31:12.580
how well you can predict.

00:31:12.580 --> 00:31:14.860
Like I said before,
there's a stochasticity

00:31:14.860 --> 00:31:18.680
in the genotype
prediction process.

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:22.010
So we want to quantify the
predictability to the leakage.

00:31:22.010 --> 00:31:25.580
So for the predictability,
what we are looking at

00:31:25.580 --> 00:31:27.130
is we have the gene
expression level.

00:31:27.130 --> 00:31:32.800
How well can you predict the
genotype from the given gene

00:31:32.800 --> 00:31:33.910
expression level?

00:31:33.910 --> 00:31:37.360
So we connect this to this
thing called "entropy."

00:31:37.360 --> 00:31:40.330
Well, you don't have to think
about it that technically

00:31:40.330 --> 00:31:44.470
but basically, higher entropy
corresponds to more randomness.

00:31:44.470 --> 00:31:46.780
And it corresponds to lower
predictability and vice

00:31:46.780 --> 00:31:48.447
versa for low productivity.

00:31:48.447 --> 00:31:50.530
So at the end of the day,
what we are trying to do

00:31:50.530 --> 00:31:53.830
is we want to come up with
a metric for predictability.

00:31:53.830 --> 00:31:57.160
That is because there
is the stochasticity

00:31:57.160 --> 00:31:59.970
in this linkage process.

00:31:59.970 --> 00:32:02.854
And then for this
information leakage,

00:32:02.854 --> 00:32:04.270
we are basically
asking, let's say

00:32:04.270 --> 00:32:06.190
we predicted this
genotype correctly

00:32:06.190 --> 00:32:08.460
and how much information
does it give me?

00:32:08.460 --> 00:32:12.010
And this is basically
the surprise

00:32:12.010 --> 00:32:15.940
of finding this genotype
within the population.

00:32:15.940 --> 00:32:19.690
And we formulate this
with this algorithm of 1

00:32:19.690 --> 00:32:21.290
over the frequency
in population.

00:32:21.290 --> 00:32:22.840
So don't worry
about this formula.

00:32:22.840 --> 00:32:25.349
What it's basically saying is
when you have higher genotype

00:32:25.349 --> 00:32:26.890
frequency-- for
example, if everybody

00:32:26.890 --> 00:32:30.250
has a certain genotype at the
variant in the population,

00:32:30.250 --> 00:32:32.290
it wouldn't really
identify anybody.

00:32:32.290 --> 00:32:36.190
For example, if I take the
brown-eyed people here,

00:32:36.190 --> 00:32:38.110
if I knew some person
was brown-eyed,

00:32:38.110 --> 00:32:39.640
I wouldn't really
identify anybody.

00:32:39.640 --> 00:32:42.910
But if I knew that one person
is green-eyed-- and usually,

00:32:42.910 --> 00:32:45.250
green-eyed is much
less frequent--

00:32:45.250 --> 00:32:50.320
than you can actually identify
that person more reliably.

00:32:50.320 --> 00:32:51.714
And again, low
genotype frequency

00:32:51.714 --> 00:32:53.380
corresponds to high
information content.

00:32:56.570 --> 00:33:00.650
So actually, if you take some
data from this 1000 Genomes

00:33:00.650 --> 00:33:04.460
Project and do
these computations

00:33:04.460 --> 00:33:10.550
for the real EQTLs, these
EQTLs meaning the variants that

00:33:10.550 --> 00:33:15.260
correlate with the
gene expression levels,

00:33:15.260 --> 00:33:17.390
the leakage unpredictability
is much higher

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:20.330
compared to a shuffled set.

00:33:20.330 --> 00:33:23.480
So the metrics that
we're proposing to use

00:33:23.480 --> 00:33:24.800
are actually meaningful.

00:33:24.800 --> 00:33:31.150
As you can see, they are much
more spread along the diagonal.

00:33:31.150 --> 00:33:34.030
And actually, if you can overlay
these allele frequencies,

00:33:34.030 --> 00:33:40.120
meaning what is the allele
frequency of that variant

00:33:40.120 --> 00:33:42.310
within the population,
then you will

00:33:42.310 --> 00:33:45.130
see that actually, the
stuff that is identifying

00:33:45.130 --> 00:33:46.930
and that is useful to
an adversary-- that

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:50.350
is, the stuff that gives
you highest leakage and that

00:33:50.350 --> 00:33:53.020
is predictable-- is going
to have moderate allele

00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:54.160
frequencies.

00:33:54.160 --> 00:33:57.100
And as expected, they
have high correlations

00:33:57.100 --> 00:34:00.370
with the gene expression levels.

00:34:00.370 --> 00:34:03.640
Well, having said
that, it's obvious

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:07.450
that an adversary wouldn't
use-- so the previous plots were

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:10.480
shown for just single EQTLs.

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:13.870
So he would actually
use multiple EQTLs.

00:34:13.870 --> 00:34:18.010
So this is where actually
the trade-off comes up.

00:34:18.010 --> 00:34:21.300
So as you can see, as
we start from the best

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:24.530
EQTL on the x-axis, we have the
predictability on the y-axis.

00:34:24.530 --> 00:34:26.389
We have the leakage.

00:34:26.389 --> 00:34:29.440
The best EQTL can be
predicted pretty well at 78%

00:34:29.440 --> 00:34:31.449
but it gives you
very small leakage.

00:34:31.449 --> 00:34:36.090
As you go up in your EQTL
list, the top two EQTLs,

00:34:36.090 --> 00:34:38.710
it gives you 50% predictable.

00:34:38.710 --> 00:34:39.820
It gives you more leakage.

00:34:39.820 --> 00:34:44.800
And at 5, you can tell there's
20% predictability and seven

00:34:44.800 --> 00:34:46.179
bits of leakage.

00:34:46.179 --> 00:34:49.750
But as you can see, an
anniversary, what he would do

00:34:49.750 --> 00:34:51.989
is start taking these guys--

00:34:51.989 --> 00:34:53.730
start using five EQTLs.

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:56.739
But although he's
gaining more information

00:34:56.739 --> 00:34:59.860
to identify individuals, he's
actually losing predictability.

00:34:59.860 --> 00:35:05.530
So this is where there is a
trade-off between how well you

00:35:05.530 --> 00:35:08.140
can predict these
genotypes and how well

00:35:08.140 --> 00:35:11.870
you can identify individuals
because of the stochasticity.

00:35:14.474 --> 00:35:16.390
So now, moving forward
to how you would really

00:35:16.390 --> 00:35:19.210
implement this in
practice, we actually

00:35:19.210 --> 00:35:26.050
propose these three steps
of a linking attack.

00:35:26.050 --> 00:35:28.840
So the first thing
is you have a person.

00:35:28.840 --> 00:35:32.950
What we want to do is we want to
select these phenotypes that we

00:35:32.950 --> 00:35:34.790
will use for attack.

00:35:34.790 --> 00:35:35.290
Task

00:35:35.290 --> 00:35:38.260
And then the next thing is
for the selected phenotypes,

00:35:38.260 --> 00:35:40.390
you do a genotype prediction.

00:35:40.390 --> 00:35:43.240
And then taking the
predicted genotypes

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:45.340
and using some
auxiliary information,

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:48.220
you can go and link these
and finally estimate

00:35:48.220 --> 00:35:53.930
the reliability of how
well you did linking.

00:35:53.930 --> 00:35:58.342
So now, I'm going to talk about
this extremity-based genotype

00:35:58.342 --> 00:35:59.800
prediction but the
idea is actually

00:35:59.800 --> 00:36:00.758
pretty straightforward.

00:36:00.758 --> 00:36:03.130
So what we are trying to
do is I take, for example,

00:36:03.130 --> 00:36:04.450
my gene expression levels.

00:36:04.450 --> 00:36:07.600
So they're my height.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:11.530
For example, let's say
we have a variant that

00:36:11.530 --> 00:36:12.830
correlates with height.

00:36:12.830 --> 00:36:16.820
So if I'm very tall
or if I'm very short,

00:36:16.820 --> 00:36:18.460
then you can actually say--

00:36:18.460 --> 00:36:21.080
these are the extremes
of that phenotype.

00:36:21.080 --> 00:36:25.060
Then you would have
a very good idea

00:36:25.060 --> 00:36:30.070
of what genotype I have
because as I showed before,

00:36:30.070 --> 00:36:31.780
these EQTLs--

00:36:31.780 --> 00:36:33.910
actually, for example,
if I'm on top here,

00:36:33.910 --> 00:36:36.100
I'm most likely
in this genotype.

00:36:36.100 --> 00:36:39.190
Or if I'm here in
the other extreme,

00:36:39.190 --> 00:36:43.720
I'm probably 0 for
this variance genotype.

00:36:43.720 --> 00:36:48.560
This is similar to if you're
driving a very expensive car.

00:36:48.560 --> 00:36:52.470
You're most likely
making a lot of money.

00:36:52.470 --> 00:36:56.320
But if you're driving
something not very expensive,

00:36:56.320 --> 00:37:03.210
you could be rich or you
may have just middle income.

00:37:03.210 --> 00:37:04.840
You may be rich
but you choose not

00:37:04.840 --> 00:37:07.390
to drive a very expensive car.

00:37:07.390 --> 00:37:10.070
This is a very
similar analogy here.

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:12.340
So what we are
basically proposing

00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:15.010
is if you're at the extremes
of these distributions,

00:37:15.010 --> 00:37:18.530
you basically assign the
extremes of the genotype.

00:37:18.530 --> 00:37:21.710
So you're either rich
or you're very poor,

00:37:21.710 --> 00:37:25.700
depending on the car
model you're driving.

00:37:25.700 --> 00:37:33.260
So when you use this
genotype prediction

00:37:33.260 --> 00:37:37.027
to do this linking attack,
it actually turns out--

00:37:37.027 --> 00:37:38.110
so don't worry about this.

00:37:38.110 --> 00:37:43.710
This is basically how we
select the EQTLs that we

00:37:43.710 --> 00:37:45.350
are using in the attack.

00:37:45.350 --> 00:37:47.160
And on the y-axis,
we have the fraction

00:37:47.160 --> 00:37:48.270
of vulnerable individuals.

00:37:48.270 --> 00:37:50.900
So what this is saying is
if you use all the EQTLs,

00:37:50.900 --> 00:37:54.430
you actually get a pretty
good linking accuracy.

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:57.020
So this is for 200 individuals.

00:37:57.020 --> 00:37:59.390
And we did a simulation
of 100,000 individuals

00:37:59.390 --> 00:38:01.970
and accuracy drops a
little bit but still, it's

00:38:01.970 --> 00:38:03.410
very close to 100%.

00:38:03.410 --> 00:38:06.860
And actually, if you had
access to that person's gender

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:10.610
or where he or she is
from, like population--

00:38:10.610 --> 00:38:13.070
or both at the same
time because gender

00:38:13.070 --> 00:38:17.430
is pretty easily predictable
from gene expression levels--

00:38:17.430 --> 00:38:20.090
and then you get actually
some improvement.

00:38:20.090 --> 00:38:22.550
If you look at here, it's
very much an improvement

00:38:22.550 --> 00:38:23.900
but it's very high already.

00:38:23.900 --> 00:38:27.860
But at these levels, there's
actually a significant increase

00:38:27.860 --> 00:38:30.330
in the accuracy.

00:38:30.330 --> 00:38:33.270
So like I said, although this is
getting a little bit technical,

00:38:33.270 --> 00:38:36.270
what we are showing here is you
can do these linking attacks

00:38:36.270 --> 00:38:40.380
with a very simple approach.

00:38:40.380 --> 00:38:43.290
Well, you could basically
actually ask, OK,

00:38:43.290 --> 00:38:47.640
this is 70% accuracy if I choose
this cut-off for selecting

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:50.940
EQTLs but which 70%?

00:38:50.940 --> 00:38:53.130
For example, I predicted
10 individuals.

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:55.230
Seven of them are right
but only the person

00:38:55.230 --> 00:38:59.940
who has the actual phenotypes
knows those seven people.

00:38:59.940 --> 00:39:03.786
Is there a way to attack or to
really identify those people?

00:39:03.786 --> 00:39:05.410
So actually, there
is a way to do that.

00:39:05.410 --> 00:39:07.890
So there is a very
simple metric.

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:10.650
We call this "first
distance gap."

00:39:10.650 --> 00:39:13.110
So for example, in our example,
we have 200 individuals.

00:39:13.110 --> 00:39:17.940
So I go and link them to the 200
individuals or old individuals

00:39:17.940 --> 00:39:20.479
like I have in the
genotype dataset.

00:39:20.479 --> 00:39:22.020
And what I do is
for each individual,

00:39:22.020 --> 00:39:23.850
I have 200 comparisons.

00:39:23.850 --> 00:39:26.850
So what I do is I sort those
200 comparisons with respect

00:39:26.850 --> 00:39:29.160
to this genotype
distance and I take

00:39:29.160 --> 00:39:31.980
the difference between the
second one and the first one.

00:39:31.980 --> 00:39:34.650
And it turns out if you
use this metric to select

00:39:34.650 --> 00:39:39.080
these individuals, you can
get a pretty high accuracy.

00:39:39.080 --> 00:39:42.510
Here, on the x-axis,
we have sensitivity.

00:39:42.510 --> 00:39:44.370
That is how many
of the individuals

00:39:44.370 --> 00:39:47.010
in the phenotype dataset
are correctly predicted.

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:51.120
On the y-axis, how many of
the people that he predicted

00:39:51.120 --> 00:39:53.130
are correctly predicted?

00:39:53.130 --> 00:39:56.510
So depending on this
d 1, 2 threshold,

00:39:56.510 --> 00:40:00.750
he can actually select a
very small number to link.

00:40:00.750 --> 00:40:03.000
And then it turns
out you can see up

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:07.600
to 40% of the individuals, you
can get 100% accuracy for this.

00:40:07.600 --> 00:40:12.660
If he used the whole
dataset, he would get 70%.

00:40:12.660 --> 00:40:14.820
But for 40% of the
individuals, you

00:40:14.820 --> 00:40:16.320
can actually get 100% accuracy.

00:40:16.320 --> 00:40:19.320
So this is 100 individuals
out of 200 dataset.

00:40:19.320 --> 00:40:22.061
This is not a small number.

00:40:22.061 --> 00:40:24.310
And then these magenta colors
are actually showing you

00:40:24.310 --> 00:40:25.830
randomly selected thresholds.

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:31.650
You can see they pretty fastly
converge to the 70% accuracy.

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:33.900
And finally, this actually
doesn't affect only

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:36.310
you but your relatives,
too, because your relatives

00:40:36.310 --> 00:40:38.730
have very high correlation
in your genotypes.

00:40:38.730 --> 00:40:41.860
That is, I take part
in, for example,

00:40:41.860 --> 00:40:46.650
genotyping service of
a company and I get

00:40:46.650 --> 00:40:48.540
a phenotype in another company.

00:40:48.540 --> 00:40:52.470
They link me but the
linking that they do,

00:40:52.470 --> 00:40:55.440
the genotype dataset doesn't
have me but my brother,

00:40:55.440 --> 00:40:56.530
for example.

00:40:56.530 --> 00:40:59.214
That actually would
put him at risk

00:40:59.214 --> 00:41:00.380
so that's what it's showing.

00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:02.210
So when you do
linking, actually, it

00:41:02.210 --> 00:41:04.380
turns out a majority
of the individuals

00:41:04.380 --> 00:41:07.440
that score in the second
rank are the relatives

00:41:07.440 --> 00:41:08.700
of the individual.

00:41:08.700 --> 00:41:10.790
So actually, when you
take part in a genotype

00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:13.290
or a phenotype study, you're
actually putting your relatives

00:41:13.290 --> 00:41:16.890
at risk, which actually creates
big, big problems about how

00:41:16.890 --> 00:41:22.870
you define consent and
all these, I guess,

00:41:22.870 --> 00:41:24.620
complicated issues.

00:41:24.620 --> 00:41:26.720
So this is the end
of my presentation.

00:41:26.720 --> 00:41:29.490
It was a little bit
technical, I guess.

00:41:29.490 --> 00:41:32.230
But if you guys
have any questions,

00:41:32.230 --> 00:41:34.148
I can try and answer them.

00:41:34.148 --> 00:41:37.501
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:44.010
In fact, for the questions
I'd, like to call Dr. Nagai up

00:41:44.010 --> 00:41:46.380
to the front as
well as Arif so we

00:41:46.380 --> 00:41:51.584
can ask the questions of
the two segment speakers.

00:41:51.584 --> 00:41:53.072
Yeah.

00:41:53.072 --> 00:41:53.572
Sure.

00:42:10.967 --> 00:42:23.610
Daisuke-- Daisuke,
astrophysics has always

00:42:23.610 --> 00:42:28.200
been at the high end
of the data generation

00:42:28.200 --> 00:42:31.380
in computational scale.

00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:35.620
You see the field starting
to skimp back on resources

00:42:35.620 --> 00:42:39.810
or are they always going to be
the most Pac-Man-like in terms

00:42:39.810 --> 00:42:43.440
of aggressively going
after whatever's available?

00:42:43.440 --> 00:42:48.450
I think I'm not seeing that
scaling back in the way that--

00:42:48.450 --> 00:42:52.215
I think that physics
and astronomy often

00:42:52.215 --> 00:42:54.930
and traditionally have
actually pushed the resources

00:42:54.930 --> 00:42:56.900
to actually grow.

00:42:56.900 --> 00:43:00.630
And for example, the
Large Hadron Collider

00:43:00.630 --> 00:43:04.300
is probably the
biggest producer of--

00:43:04.300 --> 00:43:07.170
in terms of the amount of
data that it generates,

00:43:07.170 --> 00:43:09.720
it's just sort of
redefining and it actually

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:11.820
has pushed the
infrastructure that way.

00:43:11.820 --> 00:43:13.840
I think astronomy
continues to do that.

00:43:13.840 --> 00:43:16.330
The kinds of
large-scale surveys that

00:43:16.330 --> 00:43:22.410
are coming online like LSST,
which is an optical survey

00:43:22.410 --> 00:43:25.440
to map out the distributions
of galaxies in the universe,

00:43:25.440 --> 00:43:29.550
to SKA, which is the Square
Kilometer Array in radio

00:43:29.550 --> 00:43:34.800
there are several dozens of
such surveys coming online only

00:43:34.800 --> 00:43:37.800
to produce ever more
exponentially larger

00:43:37.800 --> 00:43:40.170
amounts of data per survey.

00:43:40.170 --> 00:43:42.480
So this is going
to grow and it's

00:43:42.480 --> 00:43:47.550
going to push the envelope of
the production of big data.

00:43:47.550 --> 00:43:49.440
And it's going to
define, in terms

00:43:49.440 --> 00:43:51.240
of how to deal with
that amount of data,

00:43:51.240 --> 00:43:53.700
how to actually build
the network-- how

00:43:53.700 --> 00:43:55.020
to do the storage.

00:43:55.020 --> 00:43:59.130
I think my guess is that I
think astronomy will continue

00:43:59.130 --> 00:44:05.130
to actually experiment at this
sort of frontier of big data

00:44:05.130 --> 00:44:08.700
and do different
types of experiments

00:44:08.700 --> 00:44:13.230
and I think will help
also other fields

00:44:13.230 --> 00:44:16.174
to sort of try to produce
the best practices

00:44:16.174 --> 00:44:17.090
from such experiments.

00:44:17.090 --> 00:44:20.490
So I'm not seeing
astrophysics dialing back now.

00:44:20.490 --> 00:44:23.970
In fact, I see more
growth in terms

00:44:23.970 --> 00:44:25.650
of the amount of
data that will be

00:44:25.650 --> 00:44:27.930
produced from the
astrophysics community.

00:44:33.645 --> 00:44:35.940
So if I understood
your talk correctly,

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:38.450
one of the main
messages is that you

00:44:38.450 --> 00:44:41.880
may think you're anonymizing
your data when really, there

00:44:41.880 --> 00:44:44.970
are many ways that people can
re-extract that information.

00:44:44.970 --> 00:44:47.660
And so I wonder, with all these
initiatives like AllTrials,

00:44:47.660 --> 00:44:49.250
where they're really
pushing getting

00:44:49.250 --> 00:44:53.150
all these clinical
trial data out there,

00:44:53.150 --> 00:44:56.930
are they kind of working into
these protocols a link attack

00:44:56.930 --> 00:44:58.970
susceptibility test?

00:44:58.970 --> 00:45:01.880
This seems like it should be a
standard part of what they're

00:45:01.880 --> 00:45:04.250
doing because it may
not be obvious to them

00:45:04.250 --> 00:45:06.430
how they're putting
those patients at risk.

00:45:06.430 --> 00:45:09.590
And do you know if there
are any kind of movements

00:45:09.590 --> 00:45:12.110
to push that forward?

00:45:12.110 --> 00:45:13.890
That's right.

00:45:13.890 --> 00:45:14.390
OK.

00:45:14.390 --> 00:45:18.830
So let me say something first
before answering your question.

00:45:18.830 --> 00:45:23.930
When I do this presentation,
people usually think, like,

00:45:23.930 --> 00:45:24.860
we are terrified, OK?

00:45:24.860 --> 00:45:27.110
So what do we do about it?

00:45:27.110 --> 00:45:29.030
But the idea is not really that.

00:45:29.030 --> 00:45:30.200
It's totally the opposite.

00:45:30.200 --> 00:45:33.860
So the thing that I think
we really need to understand

00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:35.630
is these things--

00:45:35.630 --> 00:45:37.460
like, you get up.

00:45:37.460 --> 00:45:39.650
You open your window.

00:45:39.650 --> 00:45:42.219
You just gave up
some of your privacy

00:45:42.219 --> 00:45:43.760
because if somebody
was watching you,

00:45:43.760 --> 00:45:46.280
they knew that you
were at your place.

00:45:46.280 --> 00:45:50.330
It's just like when you
get out of your house,

00:45:50.330 --> 00:45:51.780
somebody can breach
your privacy.

00:45:51.780 --> 00:45:54.170
It's just whatever you
do, whatever we do,

00:45:54.170 --> 00:45:56.990
whatever data we put
out, it's going to have

00:45:56.990 --> 00:45:58.530
some sensitive information.

00:45:58.530 --> 00:46:00.570
It's about controlling the risk.

00:46:00.570 --> 00:46:03.140
So what we are trying
to come up with here

00:46:03.140 --> 00:46:07.310
is it's like we need to
find objective measures.

00:46:07.310 --> 00:46:10.130
And NIH is very
interested in that, too.

00:46:10.130 --> 00:46:12.050
So we need to find
objective measures

00:46:12.050 --> 00:46:15.770
to estimate these
risks and get it

00:46:15.770 --> 00:46:18.980
through to scientists and
public because you get consent

00:46:18.980 --> 00:46:20.060
from people.

00:46:20.060 --> 00:46:22.730
They give their data but
you actually did not really

00:46:22.730 --> 00:46:24.240
tell them what are the risks.

00:46:24.240 --> 00:46:28.580
Like you said, there are
all these initiatives.

00:46:28.580 --> 00:46:31.700
There's actually no
linkability study or something.

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:36.350
That's exactly what we were
thinking for the next step.

00:46:36.350 --> 00:46:39.290
I think to be honest, we
need a centralized way

00:46:39.290 --> 00:46:40.610
to measure these things.

00:46:40.610 --> 00:46:43.460
And we are doing open
science-- open data.

00:46:43.460 --> 00:46:47.830
That's good but you
need to also give people

00:46:47.830 --> 00:46:51.280
a perspective about what could
happen, what are the risks,

00:46:51.280 --> 00:46:54.790
and I told you guys
they're 70% predictable.

00:46:54.790 --> 00:46:56.440
What does that really mean?

00:46:56.440 --> 00:46:59.950
It's really hard to get these
things into people's minds.

00:46:59.950 --> 00:47:02.740
But in general,
medicine is like that.

00:47:02.740 --> 00:47:04.930
You tell a person,
you have a 55% chance

00:47:04.930 --> 00:47:07.090
of developing schizophrenia.

00:47:07.090 --> 00:47:09.610
Well, OK, I don't know.

00:47:09.610 --> 00:47:11.500
What does that really mean?

00:47:11.500 --> 00:47:18.260
So there is a big challenge and
I don't have the exact answer.

00:47:18.260 --> 00:47:22.010
So we need to work
more on that, I guess.

00:47:22.010 --> 00:47:23.068
That's my point.

00:47:23.068 --> 00:47:23.568
Yeah.

00:47:32.760 --> 00:47:39.860
So on the same topic, say
I give my data to 23andMe.

00:47:39.860 --> 00:47:42.690
Even if they don't
get hacked, they're

00:47:42.690 --> 00:47:44.880
obviously not going to
release all that data

00:47:44.880 --> 00:47:47.280
but anything they do release
is going to be giving up

00:47:47.280 --> 00:47:48.262
some of my privacy.

00:47:50.870 --> 00:47:53.280
So your linkage
study looks at, like,

00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:56.660
OK, if we have all of this data
available, what can we connect?

00:47:56.660 --> 00:48:00.470
But did you look at at all
what happens if they do a study

00:48:00.470 --> 00:48:02.540
and release some
bits of information

00:48:02.540 --> 00:48:04.970
and how that can affect an
individual user's privacy?

00:48:08.150 --> 00:48:09.260
We didn't do that.

00:48:09.260 --> 00:48:10.232
We didn't do that.

00:48:10.232 --> 00:48:11.190
That's a good question.

00:48:13.710 --> 00:48:19.230
Well, firstly, I think 23andMe
could release some data

00:48:19.230 --> 00:48:23.150
and you just didn't read those
user agreements well enough.

00:48:23.150 --> 00:48:24.422
Not you-- but people don't.

00:48:24.422 --> 00:48:25.380
Who reads those things?

00:48:28.290 --> 00:48:32.180
And I was actually at a
conference a month ago.

00:48:32.180 --> 00:48:34.190
There were people
from 23andMe and they

00:48:34.190 --> 00:48:37.370
were thinking about how to
open their data to researchers.

00:48:37.370 --> 00:48:42.320
So they're working towards that.

00:48:42.320 --> 00:48:44.470
And actually, they
should because that

00:48:44.470 --> 00:48:46.114
is very valuable data.

00:48:46.114 --> 00:48:47.780
There's so much to
be learned from that.

00:48:47.780 --> 00:48:51.260
But like I said, going
back, actually, they

00:48:51.260 --> 00:48:52.610
should be doing this stuff.

00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:54.530
They should be presenting
this stuff to us

00:48:54.530 --> 00:48:59.342
because it looks
like they actually--

00:48:59.342 --> 00:49:01.800
I don't want to say they don't
know what you're doing but I

00:49:01.800 --> 00:49:03.751
think they are still not there.

00:49:03.751 --> 00:49:06.000
They know what they're doing
with genotyping and stuff

00:49:06.000 --> 00:49:09.510
for sure but it's just we still
don't understand the risk.

00:49:09.510 --> 00:49:12.900
And that's a big problem because
right now, the US government is

00:49:12.900 --> 00:49:15.600
trying to recruit 1 million
people to get genotyped

00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:17.580
and that data is
going to be open.

00:49:17.580 --> 00:49:19.350
1 million people--
every 300th person--

00:49:19.350 --> 00:49:22.310
is going to be genotyped.

00:49:22.310 --> 00:49:23.757
What are the risks behind that?

00:49:23.757 --> 00:49:24.340
We don't know.

00:49:27.860 --> 00:49:32.570
I have a question about the
data getting more and more big

00:49:32.570 --> 00:49:35.870
going into big data.

00:49:35.870 --> 00:49:37.760
I saw [INAUDIBLE]
coming by that we're

00:49:37.760 --> 00:49:39.860
going from a
hypothesis-driven research

00:49:39.860 --> 00:49:42.920
to a data-driven research,
which is, of course, great.

00:49:42.920 --> 00:49:46.020
But the risk with that
is having big data,

00:49:46.020 --> 00:49:50.174
having a big n number, doing
any kind of correlation

00:49:50.174 --> 00:49:52.340
or regression with that,
you will find a correlation

00:49:52.340 --> 00:49:53.480
or regression.

00:49:53.480 --> 00:49:57.230
The problem in doing that in
astrophysics or in biology

00:49:57.230 --> 00:50:01.855
is that we don't know the data
but also not our [INAUDIBLE].

00:50:01.855 --> 00:50:03.230
If you have a
certain protein, we

00:50:03.230 --> 00:50:05.480
don't know exactly
what that protein

00:50:05.480 --> 00:50:07.610
is doing in context of
the cell where it's in

00:50:07.610 --> 00:50:09.740
and the part of
cell body it's in.

00:50:09.740 --> 00:50:15.900
So what I see coming by a lot
are these lists of things,

00:50:15.900 --> 00:50:22.410
for example, proteins with a
hit list of 20 significantly

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:26.520
changed proteins or genes
in a certain disease--

00:50:26.520 --> 00:50:29.050
most of the time,
schizophrenia or autism--

00:50:29.050 --> 00:50:32.260
and basically without
a proper understanding

00:50:32.260 --> 00:50:33.670
how does it work in a network.

00:50:33.670 --> 00:50:36.340
And then they put it in a
network and the network is huge

00:50:36.340 --> 00:50:39.340
and there's no
understanding for how

00:50:39.340 --> 00:50:43.270
that node is affecting another
node far down the network

00:50:43.270 --> 00:50:44.060
at all.

00:50:44.060 --> 00:50:49.030
So how do you make sure that
researchers are not only

00:50:49.030 --> 00:50:50.680
understanding the
data they're using

00:50:50.680 --> 00:50:54.530
but also the concept of
the data, your hypothesis,

00:50:54.530 --> 00:50:57.730
instead of actually
claiming that they

00:50:57.730 --> 00:50:59.080
have a significant result?

00:50:59.080 --> 00:51:03.190
Or probably if you collect
that data again, with that end,

00:51:03.190 --> 00:51:05.960
number you find way different
kinds of correlations.

00:51:05.960 --> 00:51:07.990
So how do you make
sure that quality

00:51:07.990 --> 00:51:13.630
that's outputted of this data
is an actual hypothesis--

00:51:13.630 --> 00:51:16.510
is an actual kind of answering
to the question instead

00:51:16.510 --> 00:51:19.435
of a data-driven approach
that just gives you

00:51:19.435 --> 00:51:24.010
a nudge in a certain direction
which is probably wrong?

00:51:24.010 --> 00:51:25.090
Who are you asking?

00:51:25.090 --> 00:51:26.300
I'm assuming?

00:51:26.300 --> 00:51:34.412
Any because both of the research
topics are gathering big data.

00:51:34.412 --> 00:51:36.860
Maybe I can comment.

00:51:36.860 --> 00:51:42.250
So in astrophysics, we do
not only one experiment

00:51:42.250 --> 00:51:45.320
to collect big data.

00:51:45.320 --> 00:51:49.210
I think part of the question
here is, how do we know that--

00:51:49.210 --> 00:51:52.720
I think there is a complexity in
the process of obtaining data,

00:51:52.720 --> 00:51:54.760
such as understanding
a telescope

00:51:54.760 --> 00:51:56.950
and how it collects
photons, registers,

00:51:56.950 --> 00:51:59.590
and all of every step of
these processes matters.

00:51:59.590 --> 00:52:01.030
And the process
of collecting data

00:52:01.030 --> 00:52:03.449
is getting ever
more complicated,

00:52:03.449 --> 00:52:05.240
not just the data is
growing but that there

00:52:05.240 --> 00:52:08.230
is a complexity in the
way you acquire the data.

00:52:08.230 --> 00:52:11.790
And one can never
be sure that there

00:52:11.790 --> 00:52:14.830
are no flaws in the
process of collecting data

00:52:14.830 --> 00:52:19.990
and that creates the
questions about the integrity

00:52:19.990 --> 00:52:23.080
of the data that's collected.

00:52:23.080 --> 00:52:25.690
Often, what we do
in astrophysics

00:52:25.690 --> 00:52:28.870
is to at least do two
or multiple experiments

00:52:28.870 --> 00:52:30.760
of the same kind.

00:52:30.760 --> 00:52:34.660
And in fact, that's what's
happening in these galaxy

00:52:34.660 --> 00:52:35.440
surveys.

00:52:35.440 --> 00:52:39.190
There are a half
dozen galaxy surveys

00:52:39.190 --> 00:52:40.600
done by different countries.

00:52:40.600 --> 00:52:44.200
Instead of joining up and
doing one bigger experiment,

00:52:44.200 --> 00:52:46.060
we're keeping them--

00:52:46.060 --> 00:52:48.910
especially in astrophysics--
separate, independent

00:52:48.910 --> 00:52:52.960
experiments so that you
can do cross-validations

00:52:52.960 --> 00:52:53.980
of different results.

00:52:53.980 --> 00:52:56.290
And often, the one team
actually claims the discovery

00:52:56.290 --> 00:53:00.260
of dark energy and the other
team may actually falsify that.

00:53:00.260 --> 00:53:01.870
Or no, but we didn't see it.

00:53:01.870 --> 00:53:05.710
So the process of checking
and doing multiple experiments

00:53:05.710 --> 00:53:06.850
is especially important.

00:53:06.850 --> 00:53:10.210
And this is what's done
often and without actually--

00:53:10.210 --> 00:53:13.600
so even within the US when
you do things like cosmic CMB

00:53:13.600 --> 00:53:16.960
experiment microwave band,
the NSF is actually--

00:53:16.960 --> 00:53:20.230
or it's important that an
agency funds not just one

00:53:20.230 --> 00:53:23.740
experiment or one that's
better but to actually think

00:53:23.740 --> 00:53:26.770
about supporting multiple
experiments exactly

00:53:26.770 --> 00:53:28.630
for this purpose.

00:53:28.630 --> 00:53:31.390
And the same goes for
computational astrophysics.

00:53:31.390 --> 00:53:34.330
When you do an experiment, we
cannot just rely on one code

00:53:34.330 --> 00:53:36.580
that is thought to be superior.

00:53:36.580 --> 00:53:39.040
But you would always want
to look for alternatives.

00:53:39.040 --> 00:53:40.480
Otherwise, we would not know.

00:53:40.480 --> 00:53:42.970
Often, we do not know
that the right answer

00:53:42.970 --> 00:53:45.370
because we are solving
this non-linear, very

00:53:45.370 --> 00:53:47.950
complicated equation which
we don't know the answers to.

00:53:47.950 --> 00:53:49.690
And that's what makes
computation great,

00:53:49.690 --> 00:53:51.670
that we can tackle
all of these questions

00:53:51.670 --> 00:53:54.130
that we didn't
know how to solve.

00:53:54.130 --> 00:53:57.160
But then because we
don't know how to solve,

00:53:57.160 --> 00:53:59.230
there's a chance that
one method will not

00:53:59.230 --> 00:54:02.380
get that right answer and only
by trying something different

00:54:02.380 --> 00:54:04.672
you would know whether you're
converging on the answer.

00:54:04.672 --> 00:54:07.088
But there is even a question
as to whether you're actually

00:54:07.088 --> 00:54:08.410
converging on the right answer.

00:54:08.410 --> 00:54:11.630
That's another deeper level
of questions about whether you

00:54:11.630 --> 00:54:14.358
got the right answer.

00:54:14.358 --> 00:54:16.346
He said the same
as what you said.

00:54:16.346 --> 00:54:17.340
[INAUDIBLE]

00:54:26.510 --> 00:54:27.050
No.

00:54:27.050 --> 00:54:27.549
No.

00:54:27.549 --> 00:54:31.550
In general, when you do
association studies like GWAS--

00:54:31.550 --> 00:54:34.000
Genome-Wide Association
Studies-- or these--

00:54:34.000 --> 00:54:37.120
I [INAUDIBLE] out
with EQTLs, genotype

00:54:37.120 --> 00:54:42.140
to phenotype correlation rather
than a binary correlation.

00:54:42.140 --> 00:54:45.260
What people do is they
have a discovery cohort

00:54:45.260 --> 00:54:49.110
and the validation cohort.

00:54:49.110 --> 00:54:50.690
So obviously, a
discovery cohort is

00:54:50.690 --> 00:54:52.610
where you find
these associations

00:54:52.610 --> 00:54:54.090
and you go and test.

00:54:54.090 --> 00:54:57.920
So there are actually tons
of statistical methods

00:54:57.920 --> 00:55:02.990
and they are very strict on how
you find a variant associating

00:55:02.990 --> 00:55:05.170
with a phenotype.

00:55:05.170 --> 00:55:07.340
And then on top of that,
you go and validate it

00:55:07.340 --> 00:55:08.960
in the validation cohort.

00:55:08.960 --> 00:55:10.640
And then on top of
that, people usually

00:55:10.640 --> 00:55:17.300
do animal models using
these CRISPR or Cas9 assays.

00:55:17.300 --> 00:55:20.420
You go and knock down the
gene or you go and change

00:55:20.420 --> 00:55:21.757
the variant--

00:55:21.757 --> 00:55:23.090
thanks to God now we can do it--

00:55:25.790 --> 00:55:28.279
and then see if there is
a corresponding change

00:55:28.279 --> 00:55:28.820
in phenotype.

00:55:28.820 --> 00:55:34.130
So there is actually a very
strict way to validate it now.

00:55:34.130 --> 00:55:36.500
It wasn't there 10
years ago but now

00:55:36.500 --> 00:55:39.710
people do it because there were
big problems with GWA studies.

00:55:39.710 --> 00:55:42.920
Now, they are much more strict.

00:55:42.920 --> 00:55:48.850
And well, this question actually
relates a lot to this topic

00:55:48.850 --> 00:55:51.010
because we are
assuming these EQTLs,

00:55:51.010 --> 00:55:52.840
those are the variants
that are changing.

00:55:52.840 --> 00:55:55.180
The expression levels
or any phenotype

00:55:55.180 --> 00:55:57.370
is universally
conserved in all people

00:55:57.370 --> 00:56:01.480
and that's not true because
depending on the population,

00:56:01.480 --> 00:56:03.275
it's actually these--

00:56:03.275 --> 00:56:05.650
for example, anything that
works in a European population

00:56:05.650 --> 00:56:06.700
may not work in--

00:56:06.700 --> 00:56:09.400
not work but any variant
that changes expression

00:56:09.400 --> 00:56:11.660
in a European population
may not change expression

00:56:11.660 --> 00:56:14.940
in an African population.

00:56:14.940 --> 00:56:17.770
But actually in our study,
I did a lot of tests

00:56:17.770 --> 00:56:20.710
like cross-comparisons
between populations

00:56:20.710 --> 00:56:22.060
and between different cohorts.

00:56:22.060 --> 00:56:27.326
It's pretty well-conserved,
so to speak.

00:56:27.326 --> 00:56:28.450
But that's a good question.

00:56:28.450 --> 00:56:30.640
These things are
major challenges

00:56:30.640 --> 00:56:32.014
for discovery of these.

00:56:35.940 --> 00:56:36.490
Yeah.

00:56:36.490 --> 00:56:39.910
Just to follow up on
the linkage risks,

00:56:39.910 --> 00:56:43.300
so given that you've
shown that there

00:56:43.300 --> 00:56:48.520
are these almost insurmountable
or unforeseeable risks, that

00:56:48.520 --> 00:56:52.930
becomes a higher and higher
hurdle for researchers

00:56:52.930 --> 00:56:55.690
to share data and
make it completely

00:56:55.690 --> 00:56:57.500
anonymous and de-identified.

00:56:57.500 --> 00:57:03.310
So would you see it as we have
to then accept some threshold

00:57:03.310 --> 00:57:05.930
of risk dependent on the data?

00:57:05.930 --> 00:57:12.130
So if my user ID for Netflix
then can be linked to me

00:57:12.130 --> 00:57:16.160
and people know that I
rated Top Gun five stars,

00:57:16.160 --> 00:57:22.690
we say, who cares or versus if
my genotype can be linked to me

00:57:22.690 --> 00:57:27.550
and that has some risk if, say,
employers or health insurance

00:57:27.550 --> 00:57:31.150
agencies or government is using
that information against me?

00:57:31.150 --> 00:57:35.410
So you have to really maintain
a higher threshold there

00:57:35.410 --> 00:57:38.350
for acceptable
risk or do we just

00:57:38.350 --> 00:57:41.860
have to accept that there's
unforeseeable consequences

00:57:41.860 --> 00:57:45.340
and there needs to be
zero, that that should

00:57:45.340 --> 00:57:49.470
be the goal is zero risk?

00:57:49.470 --> 00:57:50.500
Thanks.

00:57:50.500 --> 00:57:55.580
So first, in the Netflix
case, nobody cares about you.

00:57:55.580 --> 00:57:57.120
You rated Top Gun five.

00:57:57.120 --> 00:58:03.650
But what happens is for
example, there's a lot of stuff

00:58:03.650 --> 00:58:06.830
that people don't want to be
revealed about their movie

00:58:06.830 --> 00:58:07.640
choices--

00:58:07.640 --> 00:58:09.200
not you, not me,
but there are a lot

00:58:09.200 --> 00:58:10.700
of people who would
care about that.

00:58:10.700 --> 00:58:13.130
So that is where
actually, it's private.

00:58:13.130 --> 00:58:14.840
You don't want people
going in there.

00:58:14.840 --> 00:58:18.770
You don't want people seeing a
lot of very political movies.

00:58:18.770 --> 00:58:21.050
You rated them five or zero.

00:58:21.050 --> 00:58:24.110
Or there are a lot
of other things

00:58:24.110 --> 00:58:27.437
that could be assumed people
don't want to be seen.

00:58:27.437 --> 00:58:28.520
You want to be left alone.

00:58:28.520 --> 00:58:29.630
You want it private.

00:58:29.630 --> 00:58:35.180
So secondly, I
think yes, we have

00:58:35.180 --> 00:58:36.740
to accept that there is risk.

00:58:36.740 --> 00:58:39.140
There's always risk when
we put data out there.

00:58:39.140 --> 00:58:42.890
But in the consent
forms or something,

00:58:42.890 --> 00:58:48.220
you have to firstly objectively
and correctly predict it,

00:58:48.220 --> 00:58:52.370
estimate it, and transmit it to
the public in an intuitive way.

00:58:52.370 --> 00:58:55.160
Both of these
things are very new.

00:58:55.160 --> 00:58:56.870
We don't know how to do it.

00:58:56.870 --> 00:58:58.850
There's this
mathematical framework

00:58:58.850 --> 00:59:01.107
called "differential privacy."

00:59:01.107 --> 00:59:02.440
The computer scientists love it.

00:59:02.440 --> 00:59:03.470
It's great.

00:59:03.470 --> 00:59:05.480
But if you apply it to
the biological data,

00:59:05.480 --> 00:59:07.530
you just don't get
anything out of it.

00:59:07.530 --> 00:59:08.747
It doesn't work.

00:59:08.747 --> 00:59:09.830
A lot of companies use it.

00:59:09.830 --> 00:59:10.460
Google uses it.

00:59:10.460 --> 00:59:11.760
Microsoft uses it.

00:59:11.760 --> 00:59:13.160
But if you apply it--

00:59:13.160 --> 00:59:13.970
people did that.

00:59:13.970 --> 00:59:16.690
If you apply it to biological
data-- for example,

00:59:16.690 --> 00:59:18.630
the one paper was
actually pretty good.

00:59:18.630 --> 00:59:24.080
So they were using this
differential privacy to--

00:59:24.080 --> 00:59:26.780
so there's warfarin,
this blood thinner drug.

00:59:26.780 --> 00:59:29.240
So actually, it
turns out depending

00:59:29.240 --> 00:59:31.730
on how much warfarin
the doctors assign you,

00:59:31.730 --> 00:59:36.140
you can go and identify the
patient's genotype for it

00:59:36.140 --> 00:59:39.350
pretty certainly for
a certain variant

00:59:39.350 --> 00:59:41.540
and you don't want that
to be revealed out.

00:59:41.540 --> 00:59:44.450
And the paper basically
used differential privacy

00:59:44.450 --> 00:59:49.150
to set the warfarin
dosages but it turns out

00:59:49.150 --> 00:59:52.550
those things that were
"private" were killing patients.

00:59:52.550 --> 00:59:57.110
So actually, you just lose
all the utility of that data.

00:59:57.110 --> 01:00:00.701
So on top of that,
there's another thing.

01:00:00.701 --> 01:00:01.700
It's biological utility.

01:00:01.700 --> 01:00:04.320
How do you define the
utility of the data?

01:00:04.320 --> 01:00:05.930
So there are so many
different factors

01:00:05.930 --> 01:00:11.520
that we need to think about
in this area, I guess.

01:00:11.520 --> 01:00:14.520
Does that answer it?

01:00:14.520 --> 01:00:18.660
So Arif, it sounds
like from what

01:00:18.660 --> 01:00:23.100
you're saying that the risk is
in some sense time-dependent,

01:00:23.100 --> 01:00:27.060
that the techniques that
one has at your disposal

01:00:27.060 --> 01:00:30.660
to compare datasets or
access different datasets

01:00:30.660 --> 01:00:31.840
changes over time.

01:00:31.840 --> 01:00:35.250
So is there an obligation
upon you as a researcher

01:00:35.250 --> 01:00:41.520
to update the consent forms
for people who have given you

01:00:41.520 --> 01:00:44.760
samples and offer them
the opportunity to redact

01:00:44.760 --> 01:00:47.760
their data from your datasets?

01:00:47.760 --> 01:00:51.810
And then going back to
the open data issues

01:00:51.810 --> 01:00:57.480
that we've been discussing,
does that then cause impact

01:00:57.480 --> 01:01:01.650
on datasets that may have
been released openly in order

01:01:01.650 --> 01:01:05.730
to protect the rights and
privacy of the individuals

01:01:05.730 --> 01:01:08.542
whose samples are involved?

01:01:08.542 --> 01:01:09.500
That's a good question.

01:01:09.500 --> 01:01:10.400
Yeah.

01:01:10.400 --> 01:01:13.940
That is one of the things
that are very vague,

01:01:13.940 --> 01:01:17.660
I guess-- not vague but
it's very hard to define.

01:01:17.660 --> 01:01:20.820
Exactly-- there's
the time dependency.

01:01:20.820 --> 01:01:22.640
And actually, as a
researcher, no, I

01:01:22.640 --> 01:01:25.790
don't want to write that in a
consent form but you have to

01:01:25.790 --> 01:01:27.620
because now you will
lose a lot of people.

01:01:27.620 --> 01:01:31.090
They wouldn't want
to give their data.

01:01:31.090 --> 01:01:34.150
So it's just we need--

01:01:34.150 --> 01:01:35.860
I don't know the answer to that.

01:01:35.860 --> 01:01:39.850
I guess it should be put there
but how it should be put there

01:01:39.850 --> 01:01:44.140
and what should be there I
actually cannot really say

01:01:44.140 --> 01:01:45.596
right now.

01:01:45.596 --> 01:01:46.096
Yeah.

01:01:53.190 --> 01:01:53.690
Thank you.

01:01:53.690 --> 01:01:57.800
So both of your areas
are heavily computational

01:01:57.800 --> 01:02:00.530
and so there's a lot of
use of software and code

01:02:00.530 --> 01:02:01.700
and computation.

01:02:01.700 --> 01:02:04.940
And I was at a conference
a couple of years ago

01:02:04.940 --> 01:02:08.470
and Tony Hey made a comment
about how in the future,

01:02:08.470 --> 01:02:12.050
PIs will be working with
data scientists and software

01:02:12.050 --> 01:02:15.950
engineers And that's as much
as they work with grad students

01:02:15.950 --> 01:02:17.346
and postdocs now.

01:02:17.346 --> 01:02:19.220
So maybe if you can just
comment a little bit

01:02:19.220 --> 01:02:23.090
on that aspect of your work, how
much you rely on data science

01:02:23.090 --> 01:02:27.740
and computation and how you
see that improve the work

01:02:27.740 --> 01:02:30.561
that you're doing and inform.

01:02:33.447 --> 01:02:34.410
[INAUDIBLE]

01:02:34.410 --> 01:02:36.090
So yeah.

01:02:36.090 --> 01:02:40.710
Over the last few years,
we've had graduate students

01:02:40.710 --> 01:02:42.780
and students and
postdocs who actually

01:02:42.780 --> 01:02:44.220
move into data science.

01:02:44.220 --> 01:02:48.660
So we are one of the
producers of data scientists

01:02:48.660 --> 01:02:50.460
from physics and
astronomy because what

01:02:50.460 --> 01:02:52.440
we do is heavily
computational and skills

01:02:52.440 --> 01:02:57.060
that you learn actually are
helpful for the data science

01:02:57.060 --> 01:02:57.900
community.

01:02:57.900 --> 01:03:01.860
And at the same time,
we need to start

01:03:01.860 --> 01:03:04.320
to coordinate with
the data scientists

01:03:04.320 --> 01:03:07.610
to help develop various
aspects of the infrastructure

01:03:07.610 --> 01:03:10.900
that we need to actually
do science, as well.

01:03:10.900 --> 01:03:13.710
So I think this is starting
to happen, that in the past,

01:03:13.710 --> 01:03:17.840
I think we have
been sort of losing

01:03:17.840 --> 01:03:22.420
the people who were working
in academia to the new--

01:03:22.420 --> 01:03:24.240
generate the area
of data science

01:03:24.240 --> 01:03:28.200
but then there is now also
the convergence of working

01:03:28.200 --> 01:03:29.470
with the data scientists.

01:03:29.470 --> 01:03:31.178
And I think there has
to be a partnership

01:03:31.178 --> 01:03:33.960
between the university and
data scientists and possibly

01:03:33.960 --> 01:03:36.630
this industry to create
the infrastructure.

01:03:36.630 --> 01:03:41.460
I think that there was not
a sort of system to actually

01:03:41.460 --> 01:03:43.920
sustain the data
scientists within academia,

01:03:43.920 --> 01:03:47.860
that it was a new position
and that there was--

01:03:47.860 --> 01:03:50.160
but I think it
started to be realized

01:03:50.160 --> 01:03:52.950
that this is a very important
part of actually getting

01:03:52.950 --> 01:03:55.400
the science done.

01:03:55.400 --> 01:03:57.900
So I think that the
situation is gradually

01:03:57.900 --> 01:04:01.380
changing that there are new
data science institutes which

01:04:01.380 --> 01:04:04.020
can host and develop
the researchers.

01:04:04.020 --> 01:04:06.750
And then we have to work
with the data scientists

01:04:06.750 --> 01:04:10.830
to actually develop software
and also work on the algorithms.

01:04:10.830 --> 01:04:16.830
I envision that
data scientists are

01:04:16.830 --> 01:04:20.740
going to be a hub of different
discipline, which I think

01:04:20.740 --> 01:04:22.890
data science can work not
just with one discipline

01:04:22.890 --> 01:04:27.870
but using some techniques
and data and algorithms.

01:04:27.870 --> 01:04:29.820
If they work on
different subdisciplines,

01:04:29.820 --> 01:04:33.270
then these are the people
who actually can connect

01:04:33.270 --> 01:04:37.962
and they're sort of the nexus of
the knowledge transfer from one

01:04:37.962 --> 01:04:38.670
field to another.

01:04:38.670 --> 01:04:42.474
And I think this is a way
forward in doing innovations

01:04:42.474 --> 01:04:44.640
because, for example, some
of the techniques that we

01:04:44.640 --> 01:04:46.050
use in computational
astrophysics

01:04:46.050 --> 01:04:47.490
came from engineering.

01:04:47.490 --> 01:04:50.550
And that happened 30
years ago and we're still

01:04:50.550 --> 01:04:54.810
continuing to use the knowledge
and techniques that we

01:04:54.810 --> 01:04:56.370
inherited from other fields.

01:04:56.370 --> 01:04:59.460
And this has to happen
at a much faster speed.

01:04:59.460 --> 01:05:02.490
And I think that successfully
placing the data scientists

01:05:02.490 --> 01:05:06.480
at the nexus of your
computational and data science

01:05:06.480 --> 01:05:07.620
research in the future--

01:05:07.620 --> 01:05:10.350
and we need to think
very carefully about how

01:05:10.350 --> 01:05:13.570
to sort of make that happen.

01:05:13.570 --> 01:05:14.410
Yeah.

01:05:14.410 --> 01:05:16.810
I agree with everything he said.

01:05:16.810 --> 01:05:18.130
I want to add just one thing.

01:05:18.130 --> 01:05:22.760
So well, the way I think
about data science,

01:05:22.760 --> 01:05:26.540
I still don't have a real exact
definition of it in my mind.

01:05:26.540 --> 01:05:30.850
But I would say--

01:05:30.850 --> 01:05:34.450
just totally kind
of in parallel but--

01:05:34.450 --> 01:05:39.250
85% of the time that we
are spending to do analysis

01:05:39.250 --> 01:05:44.500
is just gathering
data, getting rid of--

01:05:44.500 --> 01:05:47.950
reformatting it, getting
rid of the noise, the stuff

01:05:47.950 --> 01:05:50.920
that you don't need, and
getting to the final thing

01:05:50.920 --> 01:05:53.440
that is maybe a data
structure or an Excel file.

01:05:53.440 --> 01:05:56.350
It may be something in
your computer memory

01:05:56.350 --> 01:05:59.270
but getting to the point
where you can now apply

01:05:59.270 --> 01:06:01.140
some machine learning on it.

01:06:01.140 --> 01:06:04.300
But the problem is
genomics, at least,

01:06:04.300 --> 01:06:07.960
is going to a place where
you just cannot fit something

01:06:07.960 --> 01:06:08.470
like--

01:06:08.470 --> 01:06:10.270
right now, we have
1,000 Genomes Project.

01:06:10.270 --> 01:06:15.580
You had 3 million variants,
2,500 individuals--

01:06:15.580 --> 01:06:16.630
gigabytes of memory.

01:06:16.630 --> 01:06:17.260
Fine.

01:06:17.260 --> 01:06:20.510
But now there's going
to be 30,000 individuals

01:06:20.510 --> 01:06:22.660
and then there's going to
be 1 million individuals.

01:06:22.660 --> 01:06:24.760
There's just not enough memory.

01:06:24.760 --> 01:06:29.710
It just starts going into
what he was going to.

01:06:29.710 --> 01:06:35.020
It's going to a place where you
have to do everything either

01:06:35.020 --> 01:06:37.720
like HPC-level distributed--

01:06:37.720 --> 01:06:39.240
how do you design
those algorithms?

01:06:39.240 --> 01:06:41.710
You need a lot of
data scientists,

01:06:41.710 --> 01:06:45.950
I guess, for data wrangling,
data cleaning and stuff.

01:06:45.950 --> 01:06:48.580
And you need people in
biology that understand

01:06:48.580 --> 01:06:50.600
the output of those algorithms.

01:06:50.600 --> 01:06:56.470
So it's becoming a really
multidisciplinary science.

01:06:56.470 --> 01:07:01.066
And I think the
future is going to be

01:07:01.066 --> 01:07:03.190
we are going to do everything
on the clock you know

01:07:03.190 --> 01:07:05.110
because there's just
there's no way we

01:07:05.110 --> 01:07:11.440
can fit 1 million genomes and
the whole row dataset even

01:07:11.440 --> 01:07:12.550
on HPC.

01:07:12.550 --> 01:07:14.426
We just don't have
that hardware and we

01:07:14.426 --> 01:07:16.300
don't want to have that
hardware because it's

01:07:16.300 --> 01:07:18.460
really hard to keep that up.

01:07:18.460 --> 01:07:21.590
We go back and forth with
these guys every week.

01:07:21.590 --> 01:07:24.920
We piss them off and
then they piss us off.

01:07:24.920 --> 01:07:30.250
But I think the
way we have things

01:07:30.250 --> 01:07:32.987
is not very
sustainable and yeah,

01:07:32.987 --> 01:07:34.945
I think it's going to be
very multidisciplinary

01:07:34.945 --> 01:07:35.748
in the future.

01:07:39.640 --> 01:07:42.110
I have one more question.

01:07:42.110 --> 01:07:44.510
It's a question about CRC--

01:07:44.510 --> 01:07:46.450
very happy about that.

01:07:46.450 --> 01:07:48.430
And I'm a computational
neuroscientist.

01:07:48.430 --> 01:07:53.200
I'm actually quite new
here, hence my accent.

01:07:53.200 --> 01:07:57.995
And I looked through the
site and really, well, I

01:07:57.995 --> 01:08:00.200
signed up for a couple
of things-- waiting list.

01:08:00.200 --> 01:08:02.680
But what would really
help me at the moment

01:08:02.680 --> 01:08:08.170
is to have someone or
one on one or a group

01:08:08.170 --> 01:08:13.210
to discuss some models,
some computational tactics,

01:08:13.210 --> 01:08:15.550
or some data structures.

01:08:15.550 --> 01:08:17.979
And I looked through the
website and I couldn't find it.

01:08:17.979 --> 01:08:19.120
Maybe I overlooked that.

01:08:19.120 --> 01:08:21.370
But is there an initiative
or people planning

01:08:21.370 --> 01:08:23.802
to do that as a kind of meet-up?

01:08:23.802 --> 01:08:25.160
Over here [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:25.160 --> 01:08:26.190
Great.

01:08:26.190 --> 01:08:26.689
Great.

01:08:26.689 --> 01:08:27.189
Thank you.

01:08:27.189 --> 01:08:28.665
That's all I want to know.

01:08:40.040 --> 01:08:42.380
I'm so glad you brought that
up and I would absolutely

01:08:42.380 --> 01:08:47.330
encourage you to reach out to
either Dr. Rob Bjornson or Dr.

01:08:47.330 --> 01:08:49.609
Andy Sherman regarding that.

01:08:49.609 --> 01:08:54.879
That's why they're here and
they love helping people out.

